  "page_text": "                                                                                    \n                                                                                    \n                                                                                    \n     START  UP                             If the pump still does not deliver flow, the cause may be one\n                                           or more of the following:                \n     Before starting the pump, check the following: 1. Suction line air leaks. Vacuum gauge reading should help\n     1. Are there vacuum and pressure gauges on or near the determine if this is the problem.\n       pump? These gauges are the quickest and most accurate 2. End of suction pipe not submerged deep enough in liquid.\n       way of finding out what is happening in the pump.                            \n                                           3. Suction lift is too great or the suction piping is too small.\n     2. Check alignment - See suggestions in the Alignment                          \n                                           4. Liquid is vaporizing in the suction line before it\n       section of this manual.                                                      \n                                             gets to the pump.                      \n     3. Check piping to be sure there is no strain on the                           \n                                           If after consideration of these points it still does not pump,\n       pump casing.                                                                 \n                                           review again all points under START UP. Read through\n     4. Rotate the pump shaft by hand to be sure it turns freely. Troubleshooting in this manual and try again. If it still does\n       MAKE SURE THE PUMP DRIVER IS LOCKED OUT OR not pump, contact your Viking Pump® representative.\n       CANNOT BE ENERGIZED BEFORE DOING THIS.                                       \n     5. Jog motor to be sure it is turning in the right direction; refer            \n                                           TROUBLESHOOTING                          \n       to discussion on pump rotation under item 4 in General                       \n       Installation Notes section.                                                  \n                                           A Viking pump that is properly installed and maintained will\n     6. Check any relief valves to be sure they are installed give long and satisfactory performance.\n       correctly. Refer to discussion on relief valves in General                   \n                                           NOTE: Before making any pump adjustment or opening the\n       Installation Notes section.                                                  \n                                           pump liquid chamber in any manner, make sure that:\n     7. Check suction piping to be sure:                                            \n                                           1. Any pressure in the pumping chamber has been vented\n       a. It is all connected and tight      through the suction or discharge lines or other openings\n       b. Valves are open                    provided for this purpose.             \n       c. End of pipe is below liquid level 2. The driver has been “locked out” so that it cannot\n                                             inadvertently be started while work is being done\n     8. Check discharge piping to be sure:                                          \n                                             on the pump.                           \n       a. It is all connected and tight                                             \n                                           3. The pump has been allowed to cool down to the point\n       b. Valves are open                    where there is no chance of anyone being burned.\n       c. There is a place for the liquid to go If trouble does develop, one of the first steps toward finding\n     9. Lubricate any grease fitting on the pump using a #2 NLGI the difficulty is to install a vacuum gauge in the suction port\n       grease. Check any gear reducer, motor, coupling, etc. and a pressure gauge in the discharge port. Readings on\n       for instructions and lubricate as recommended by the these gauges often will give a clue as to where to start looking\n       manufacturer. See Engineering Service Bulletin for the trouble.              \n       ESB-515 at the end of the Appendix for Viking standard                       \n                                           VACUUM  GAUGE - SUCTION PORT             \n       grease types to check compatibility.                                         \n     10. For packed pumps, loosen packing gland nuts so gland 1. High reading would indicate:\n       can be moved slightly by hand. Adjust gland to reduce a. Suction line is blocked by a stuck foot valve,\n       leakage only after pump has run long enough to reach stuck gate valve, or plugged strainer.\n       constant temperature. Packing should weep a little to keep                   \n                                             b. Liquid is too viscous to flow through the piping.\n       it cool and lubricated.                                                      \n                                             c. Lift is too high.                   \n     11. Do not use the Viking pump to flush, pressure test or                      \n       prove the system with water. Either remove the pump or d. Line is too small. \n       run piping around it while flushing or testing. Pumping 2. Low reading would indicate:\n       water, dirty or otherwise, can do more damage in a few                       \n                                             a. Air leak in suction line.           \n       minutes than months of normal service.                                       \n                                             b. End of pipe is not in liquid.       \n     12. Check to be sure all guards are in place.                                  \n                                             c. Pump is worn.                       \n     13. Check the pump to be sure it is heated to operating                        \n       temperature (if jacketed or heat traced). d. Pump is dry - should be primed. \n     If the pump begins to deliver liquid within 60 seconds, it 3. Fluttering, jumping, or erratic reading:\n     can continue to be operated. If liquid is not leaving the a. Liquid is vaporizing.\n     discharge port, stop the pump. Running the pump longer than                    \n                                             b. Liquid is coming to pump in slugs, possibly an air leak,\n     one minute without liquid inside it can damage the pump.                       \n                                               insufficient liquid above the end of the suction pipe.\n     Review the steps just outlined, consider what the suction                      \n     and discharge gauges indicate, and see Troubleshooting c. Vibrating from cavitation, misalignment,\n                                               or damaged parts.                    \n     section. If everything appears to be in order, put some liquid                 \n     in the pump. This will help it prime.                                          \n     The pump can be restarted. If nothing is flowing within two                    \n     minutes, stop the pump. The pump is not a compressor; it will                  \n     not build up much air pressure. It may be necessary to vent                    \n     the discharge line until liquid begins to flow.                                \n              NOTE: 226A SERIES™ & 4226A SERIES™ JACKETED PUMPS DISCONTINUED AS OF 4Q20.\n     TSM 1600 | Issue C | Page 16 of 20               © 2020 Viking Pump, Inc. • Cedar Falls, IA",
